  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "oIfeVsa1ce5giNvu5B02t1vlyFo=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:32.859Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Atol CD delivers Alfresco, Maarch Courrier and OnlyOffice \u2014 a document stack. Together these systems move documents through an organisation and each keeps its own view of where a document currently sits, which is what a document-flow tool is for.\n\nWhat none of them keeps is a decision record. The distinction is between a document's journey and the acts performed along it: a document system can show that a file reached a department and that a response exists, without asserting that an official decided something, on what authority, at what moment. The trail describes movement, and audit asks about judgement.\n\nThe research labels this the same Alfresco gap identified elsewhere in the corpus, the difference being the delivery base \u2014 French public-sector clients. That repetition is the useful part: the gap belongs to the document-management category rather than to one firm, so an argument constructed once transfers across the Alfresco leads, while the French public-sector footprint decides where it is worth making first.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- Atol CD (FR Dijon +5) \u2014 not published \u00b7 contact@atolcd.com \u00b7 https://atolcd.com\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Alfresco, Maarch Courrier and OnlyOffice \u2014 document flow with no decision record. Same Alfresco gap, with a French public-sector delivery base.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:28:32.858998Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:32.859001Z"
    }
  },
